            <div style="border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); border-radius: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; overflow: hidden;"><div style="padding: 16px 20px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); cursor: pointer;"><strong style="color: #fff;">How much water should I store for a hurricane?</strong></div><div style="padding: 0 20px 16px; color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7); line-height: 1.8;">Store at least two gallons of water per person per day for a minimum of seven days. A family of four needs a minimum of 56 gallons. One gallon is for drinking and the second for sanitation and cooking. Hurricanes can disrupt water treatment plants and break mains for one to two weeks, so the extended supply is critical compared to other disaster types.</div></div>
            <div style="border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); border-radius: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; overflow: hidden;"><div style="padding: 16px 20px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); cursor: pointer;"><strong style="color: #fff;">Why should hurricane supplies last 7 days instead of 3?</strong></div><div style="padding: 0 20px 16px; color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7); line-height: 1.8;">Hurricanes cause extended infrastructure damage that takes longer to repair than most other disasters. Flooded roads prevent supply trucks from reaching affected areas, power restoration after major hurricanes can take two to three weeks, and water treatment facilities may be offline for over a week. Seven days is the realistic minimum for self-sufficiency.</div></div>
            <div style="border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); border-radius: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; overflow: hidden;"><div style="padding: 16px 20px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); cursor: pointer;"><strong style="color: #fff;">Is it safe to use a generator during a hurricane?</strong></div><div style="padding: 0 20px 16px; color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7); line-height: 1.8;">Never run a generator indoors, in a garage, or near windows and doors. Generator exhaust contains carbon monoxide, which kills hundreds of people annually. Place generators at least 20 feet from your home with the exhaust pointing away. Never connect a generator to your homes wiring without a transfer switch. Fill the fuel tank before the storm and store extra fuel safely away from the generator.</div></div>
            <div style="border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); border-radius: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; overflow: hidden;"><div style="padding: 16px 20px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); cursor: pointer;"><strong style="color: #fff;">When should I evacuate versus shelter in place?</strong></div><div style="padding: 0 20px 16px; color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7); line-height: 1.8;">Always follow mandatory evacuation orders. Evacuate if you are in a flood zone, storm surge area, or mobile home. Shelter in place only if you are in a sturdy building outside evacuation zones and authorities have not ordered evacuation. Leave early, as traffic congestion and road closures make late evacuation dangerous.</div></div>
            <div style="border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); border-radius: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; overflow: hidden;"><div style="padding: 16px 20px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); cursor: pointer;"><strong style="color: #fff;">What should I do if flooding starts during the hurricane?</strong></div><div style="padding: 0 20px 16px; color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7); line-height: 1.8;">Move to the highest level of your home immediately. Never walk, swim, or drive through floodwater. Just six inches of moving water can knock you down and two feet can sweep away a vehicle. If water rises to the first floor, move to the attic or roof and signal for rescue. Avoid contact with floodwater, which may contain sewage, chemicals, and debris.</div></div>
